{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790508778,"nonce":"VZ2Kpxo0quKcAaPOhqxULNr+itU=","message":"2026-09-27 implementation survey and corrected boundary: Zyntax's current wasm_backend emits standalone runtime modules for individual HIR functions. It does not emit relocatable wasm objects that Ash's linker can consume. Its LLVM AOT-via-object path emits a host object, links a host dylib, and dlopens it; it has no portable target/object API for wasm32 yet.\\n\\nCaribou's AOT coordinator is being changed to collect language artifacts and pass their objects to Ash's one final linker. A declared frontend with no object emitter is refused by name instead of being omitted or interpreted. To complete this issue, Zyntax needs a target-aware relocatable object emitter, caribou::link exports, and a frontend call adapter for each supported machine type. Python, Lua, and ZynML then use that same artifact contract.\\n\\nThe earlier notes about handing final Zyntax wasm modules to a custom linker and statically linking .zrtl plugins are superseded: Ash owns the final link, and native plugins/.zrtl dependencies remain side modules outside the language-object graph.","files":null}]}